# Course-morning pools, group courses_upper (docs/authoring/pools.md):
# two more events each at levels II and III for the six core courses. Repeatable routine events.

# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation, skill arcana) ----------------

@event evo_rain_barrel
title: Rain on Demand
track: evocation
level: 2
lesson: mirror_lake
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: rain
tags: class magic
scene: A row of students on a lakeside terrace, each with a small grey cloud above a wooden barrel, one cloud raining sideways onto a neighbour.
text: {Tidecalling at Evocation II. Pull a cloud off the lake, park it over a barrel, and fill the barrel to the chalk line. Not over, not under.|The barrels are out on the terrace again, each with a chalk line.|You know your barrel now, the one with the iron band that's slipped.} {Professor Marisol is reading on the steps, under a perfectly dry patch of air.|The wind keeps trying to borrow the clouds.|{season == "winter": The clouds come off the lake as sleet.|A heron watches the barrels with professional interest.}}
> left: Wring it out to the line
  challenge spell standard steady:
    win:
      arcana +small
      study evocation +tiny
      say: {The last drop lands on the chalk. Marisol looks up from her book and back down, which counts.|You stop the rain a breath early and let the drip finish the job. Exactly to the line.|You know how much your barrel leaks at the slipped band, and you rain that much extra. Exactly to the line.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {Your cloud overshoots, fills the barrel, and keeps going down your collar.|The cloud drifts left and rains on the next barrel, which is not yours, and on its owner, who is very not yours. The splashback finds your collar anyway.|The heron moves at the wrong moment and so does your cloud. It empties itself over the heron, who leaves offended.}
> right: Drizzle it in, slowly
  arcana +small
  mana -small
  mood -tiny
  say: {It takes the whole lesson. Your cloud is the size of a pillow by the end, and very tired.|You fill it a thimble at a time. The others have gone in for lunch. The line is met.|You drizzle it in while your barrel leaks it out at the band. It's a long lesson, but you win in the end.}

@event evo_ember_stair
title: Carrying the Ember
track: evocation
level: 2
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: flame
tags: class magic danger
scene: A student climbing a dim spiral stair, a single glowing ember resting in their bare open palm.
text: {Pyromancy at Evocation II. Carry a live ember in your bare palm from the ground floor to the top of the Lecture Tower. Keep it alight. Keep your hand.|The ember drill again. The stair hasn't got any shorter.|Ember day. You've counted the stair by now, and you know where the draught is.} {A door on the third floor opens just as you pass.|Someone ahead of you is climbing very fast and swearing quietly.|The lamps on the stair are out, and your ember is the only light.}
> left: Feed it, and climb fast
  challenge feat using arcana standard:
    win:
      arcana +small
      say: {You keep the ember bright with small breaths of heat and take the stairs two at a time. It arrives fat and orange.|You reach the top with the ember glowing and your palm only warm. The lecturer tips it into a brazier without a word.|Your ember lights the dark stair all the way up. People wait on the landings to follow you.}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {The ember flares on the landing and you learn exactly how hot a palm can get. You arrive with it alight, and with a blister.|You feed it too much at the draughty window. It bites. You carry it up the last flight in your other hand.|You feed it in the dark and misjudge it. It bites, and you finish the climb by its light and your own swearing.}
> right: Shield it, and climb slow
  study evocation +small
  mood -small
  say: {You climb at a crawl with the ember cupped against the draughts. It is alight at the top, just. So are your nerves.|Step by step, a held breath at each window. Last up, but the ember's still glowing.|You shield it from the draught you know is coming. It reaches the top small and steady, and so do you.}

@event evo_firebreak
title: The Reed Burn
track: evocation
level: 3
lesson: mirror_lake
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: flame
tags: class magic danger
scene: Advanced students along a lake shore in a line, half of them calling a low wall of fire through dry reeds, half of them holding back a curtain of lake water behind it.
text: {Advanced Evocation does real work, the controlled burn of the dead reeds along the lake shore. Pyromancers lead the fire, tidecallers hold the line behind it.|Burn day. The shore smells of smoke for a week afterwards.|The reed burn. You can tell a fire's mood from its smoke now.} {The wind is steady from the lake.|The wind can't decide.|The reeds are drier than anyone would like.} {season == "summer": The air is hot already.|Your breath steams.}
> left: Walk at the fire's head
  challenge spell standard+1:
    win:
      arcana +medium
      say: {The fire follows your hands down the shore like a dog on a lead. The lecturer lets you take it all the way to the boathouse.|You lead the fire low and even, and it goes out where you tell it. Behind you there's nothing but clean black ground.|You lead the fire through the dry reeds slowly and keep it on a short lead. Not a spark gets away.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -small
      say: {A gust lifts the fire over your head. The tidecallers drop it in a wall of lake water, and you with it.|The fire jumps the line and runs. It's caught in a minute, but your eyebrows weren't.|The dry reeds go up faster than you can lead them. The tidecallers save the boathouse; you save some of your sleeve.}
> right: Hold the water line
  study evocation +small
  mana -small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You stand in the shallows holding a curtain of lake water for an hour. Nothing gets past you. Nobody notices, which is the point.|Your arms ache from keeping the water up. It's the least glamorous job on the shore and the one the lecturer checks first.|You hold the water line through the worst of it. When the dry reeds flare, your curtain is the one that holds.}

@event evo_drowned_flame
title: Fire Under Water
track: evocation
level: 3
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: class magic
scene: A glass tank of water on a scorched bench, a small blue flame burning steadily at the bottom of it.
text: {Original work in Advanced Evocation, a fire that burns underwater. Every student gets a tank. Most tanks get a new pane every week.|Your tank is waiting, with a fresh crack along one pane.|The tank again. You talk to the water now, and to the fire, separately.} {Someone's tank has boiled dry and they're staring at it.|A flame at the next bench burns green beneath the water, quite calm.|The lecturer is standing well back today.}
> left: Light it at the bottom
  challenge spell standard+1 wild:
    win:
      arcana +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {A small blue flame takes hold under the water and stays. The bubbles go up in a thin silver line.|It catches, gutters, and settles. The water around it doesn't even warm. You don't know how you did it.|You light it while the lecturer stands well back. It burns, small and stubborn, and the lecturer comes a step closer.}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {The water boils in a heartbeat and the pane splits. You wear the steam on your face for the rest of the day.|The flame goes up instead of in. The tank cracks, and your sleeve is wet and scorched at once.|You learn why the lecturer is standing back. The pane goes, and so does your eyebrow.}
> right: Wrap it in a bubble first
  arcana +small
  mana -small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You hold a skin of air around the flame and sink them both. It's half a cheat, and the lecturer says so. It burns all hour.|Your flame sits in a bubble at the bottom like a moth in a jar. Not the assignment, but not nothing.|You wrap it in a bubble, as you always do now. The lecturer says it's time to try without, and doesn't make you.}

# ---------------- Astromancy & Divination (track: astromancy, skill lore) ----------------

@event astro_sealed_forecast
title: Tomorrow's Weather
track: astromancy
level: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: rain
tags: class study
scene: A row of sealed envelopes pinned to a corkboard in the observatory, each labelled with a student's name and tomorrow's date.
text: {Foresight at Astromancy II. Write down tomorrow's weather to the hour, seal it, pin it to the board. The lecturer opens the envelopes a day later, in public.|The envelopes come out again.|You've written so many forecasts you dream in drizzle.} {Outside the dome the sky is giving nothing away.|Last week's best forecast just said "Wet", and was right.|{season == "winter": Frost on the dome, and snow somewhere in the future.|Clouds over the lake, moving with intent.}}
> left: Commit to the hour
  challenge research standard wild:
    win:
      lore +small
      study astromancy +tiny
      say: {"Rain at the third bell, stopping at the fourth." It does. The lecturer reads it out twice.|You forecast a fog to the hour. When the envelope is opened, the fog is pressing on the dome.|You write "Sleet at the second bell, from the lake." It comes off the lake at the second bell. The lecturer stops reading the others.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {"Clear by noon." It rains till evening. Your envelope is read out to great enjoyment.|You predicted wind. Tomorrow turns out so still that a feather on the sill doesn't move.|You forecast with great confidence. Tomorrow is the opposite of everything you wrote, in order.}
> right: Hedge, and learn from it
  study astromancy +small
  say: {"Some weather, at some point." The lecturer reads it out flatly and moves on. But you spent the hour watching how the good forecasters work.|You write a careful forecast with three possibilities. It's right, technically. The lecturer calls it "an umbrella of a prophecy".|You hedge, and read the others' envelopes after. You can tell the good ones by how short they are.}

@event astro_stubborn_telescope
title: The Stubborn Telescope
track: astromancy
level: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: planet
tags: class study
scene: A student tugging at a huge brass telescope in a dome, while it points stubbornly at a different patch of sky.
text: {Astromancy II needs exact positions, and that means a night on the great telescope. At the morning lecture your name goes up on tonight's rota. The great telescope, everyone warns you, points wherever it likes.|Your name is on tonight's telescope rota again. The lecturer warns you it is already pointing at something else.|Your night on the telescope again. The lecturer only raises an eyebrow at you as the rota goes up.} {This week it wants the moon.|This week it's fixed on the lake, which is not in the sky.|This week it keeps drifting east, slowly, like a sigh.}
> left: Argue it round
  challenge research standard:
    win:
      lore +small
      say: {You coax it, inch by inch, and it settles on your star with a creak like a shrug.|You find the right thing to say to an old brass instrument. It points where you ask. You write the position fast.|You coax it off its eastward drift with a gentle hand and a sharp word. It sighs back to your star.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {It spends the night pointing where it likes. So do you, in the end, cold and annoyed.|You lean on it. It leans back. You go to bed at the fourth bell with nothing written down.|It drifts east all night, slowly, and takes you with it. You write down a very good position for the wrong star.}
> right: Work it out from tables
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: {You leave the telescope to its moods and work the positions out by candlelight from the old tables. It takes until dawn.|Pages of sums by candle. The telescope watches over your shoulder, pointedly.|You use the tables and let the telescope drift where it likes. Your positions are good, and you're in bed by midnight.}

@event astro_long_watch
title: Keeping the Light
track: astromancy
level: 3
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: lantern
tags: class magic
scene: A student on the observatory roof before dawn, a glass lantern in their lap holding a single cold point of starlight.
text: {Advanced Starlight. At the morning lecture you're given tonight's task. Hold one star's light in a lantern through a whole night on the roof. It will want to go home before dawn. Your job is to keep it.|The night watch on the roof comes round again. After the morning lecture you collect a lantern and a blanket.|Your lantern, your star, your corner of the roof, booked for tonight.} {The star you've drawn is pale and blue.|The star you've drawn is small and stubborn.|The star you've drawn is very old and very faint.} {season == "winter": There will be frost on the slates.|The night will be warm and full of moths.}
> left: Keep it all night
  lore +small
  study astromancy +small
  health -small
  mood -tiny
  say: {At dawn your star is still in the lantern, blinking at the sun it was never meant to meet. You are blinking too.|You keep it till the lecturer comes up at sunrise. Your eyes are full of sand and your lantern is full of light.|Your old faint star almost goes out at the fourth bell. You talk to it until dawn, and it stays.}
> right: Let it go at midnight
  lore +tiny
  say: {You open the lantern and the light goes up like a kite let loose. You watch it all the way home and then go to bed.|You let it go early. The lecturer's note just says "Released at midnight." You suspect it's not a compliment.|You let the old star go at midnight. It goes slowly, as if it isn't sure of the way. You watch it until you can't.}
> up[starlight]: Feed it your own starlight
  lore +small
  say: {You pour a little of your own starlight into the lantern. The star brightens as if it had been lonely.|Your light and the star's together are enough to read by. You read until dawn.|You give the old star some of your light. It burns brighter than it has in a long time, and you feel it go out of you.}

@event astro_two_futures
title: Both Futures
track: astromancy
level: 3
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: dice
tags: class magic
scene: A coin balanced on edge on a dark table in the observatory, with two faint ghostly outcomes hovering above it.
text: {Advanced Foresight. The lecturer balances a coin on its edge. Read both futures at once, the one where it falls heads and the one where it falls tails, and hold them both.|The coin on its edge again. You know how it feels to be two places at once now.|The coin. People come out of this lesson pressing their temples.} {Someone in the front row says "heads" in two voices.|The lecturer is drinking tea and looking at nobody.|The candles burn straight up, very still.}
> left: Hold both to the end
  challenge exam standard+1:
    win:
      lore +medium
      say: {For a long moment you live both futures, side by side, and neither is frightening. Then the coin falls, and one of them goes out.|You hold both until the lecturer flicks the coin. One future folds away, neat as a letter.|You hold both in the still candlelight until the lecturer stops the coin with a finger. Both futures are still there. "Interesting," the lecturer says.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -small
      say: {The futures tangle and you get a headache that lasts till supper. For an hour you keep reaching for the wrong door handle.|You lose track of which future is which, and of which way is down. The lecturer sits you by an open window.|You hold both too long. For the rest of the day you hear things a moment before they happen, and you hate it.}
> right: Read one, then the other
  study astromancy +small
  mana -small
  say: {You read heads, then tails, carefully, one after the other. It's not the exercise, but you get both right.|One future at a time. Slower, safer, and the lecturer says you'll get there.|One future, then the other, and you know both now before you read them. The lecturer says that's the start of holding them.}

# ---------------- Alchemy & Healing (track: alchemy, skill alchemy) ----------------

@event alch_unlabelled_vial
title: The Unlabelled Vial
track: alchemy
level: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: bottle
tags: class study
scene: A vial of clear liquid on a lab bench beside a strip of paper turning slowly purple, a student squinting at both.
text: {Alchemy II hands out an unlabelled vial each, and an hour to say what's in it. Paper strips, a candle, a nose and a notebook are allowed.|Another unlabelled vial. The lecturer enjoys these.|The vial is waiting on your bench. You've started recognising the corks.} {Yours is perfectly clear and smells faintly of pears.|Yours is cloudy and keeps settling into layers.|Yours is warm to the touch.} {The ventilation charm is wheezing again.|The lab is quiet except for scratching quills.|The lecturer is humming, which means the vials are harder this week.}
> left: Test it the long way
  alchemy +small
  mood -small
  say: {Strip, flame, cold, filter. By the end of the hour you've a name and three reasons for it.|Every test, in order, written up neatly. Your notes are dull and your answer is right.|The warm one takes the whole hour, test by test. It's something you've never met, and you name it anyway, correctly.}
> right: Trust your nose
  challenge brew standard wild:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      study alchemy +tiny
      say: {One sniff and you know it. You write the name and use the spare hour to check yourself.|You tip the vial under your nose and the answer arrives before the smell does. The lecturer raises an eyebrow and a tick.|It's warm, and that tells you most of it. You sniff to confirm. The lecturer stops humming.}
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {One sniff and your eyes stream for the rest of the morning. You guessed wrong, too.|It's not what you thought, and it is very much up your nose. You spend the hour at the window.|You sniff the warm one. It sniffs back, in a way, and you spend the hour with a damp cloth on your face.}

@event alch_cracked_tray
title: The Cracked Tray
track: alchemy
level: 2
lesson: infirmary
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: heart
tags: class magic
scene: A tray in the infirmary laid with a cracked teacup, a cracked hand mirror and a cracked bone from a teaching skeleton, a student's hands hovering over them.
text: {Mend at Alchemy II. Sister Amaranth sets out a tray of cracked things, cup, mirror and bone. "Mend those," she says, "and then we'll see about people."|The tray again. Sister Amaranth has found new things to crack.|Sister Amaranth puts the tray down and nods at it. You know the drill.} {A second-year is waiting on a cot with a sprained wrist from broom practice.|A third-year with a scalded hand from Evocation is waiting.|A first-year is in with a split lip and a long story about a door.}
> left: Mend the tray, perfectly
  challenge enchant standard:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      say: {The cracks close like seams being sewn shut. Sister Amaranth runs a thumbnail along the cup and finds nothing.|Cup, mirror, bone, whole. Sister Amaranth says "Fine," which is her highest word.|You mend the tray in half the time and Sister Amaranth sends you to the first-year with the split lip. It mends cleanly.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {The cup mends with a ridge in it, the mirror with a ripple. Sister Amaranth sets them on the shelf of shame.|You mend the bone a little crooked. Sister Amaranth says that's how you learn and puts it back in the skeleton anyway.|The mirror mends with your face in it, slightly wrong. Sister Amaranth turns it to the wall.}
> right: Mend the patient instead
  study alchemy +small
  mana -small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You skip the tray and take the patient, with Sister Amaranth's hand on your shoulder. It goes slowly. It works.|Sister Amaranth lets you try on the patient while she watches. You're shaky and slow, and you'll never mend a cup the same way again.|You mend the first-year's lip and hear the whole story about the door. It wasn't a door. You keep that to yourself.}

@event alch_lake_fever
title: Lake Fever Season
track: alchemy
level: 3
lesson: infirmary
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: potion
tags: class magic
scene: A row of infirmary cots, students under blankets looking flushed and sorry for themselves, an advanced student drawing a faint grey haze out of one patient's brow.
text: {Advanced Alchemy treats real patients. The infirmary has Lake Fever cases, from swimming where they shouldn't. Sister Amaranth wants the fever drawn out, not just cooled.|Lake Fever again. Students never learn, Sister Amaranth says, and neither do lakes.|Fever duty. Sister Amaranth hands you a list without looking up.} {Your patient says they only went in up to the knees.|Your patient is a prefect, very embarrassed.|Your patient insists they were pushed.}
> left: Draw the fever out by hand
  challenge enchant standard+1:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      study alchemy +small
      say: {The fever comes out like a grey thread pulled from wool. Your patient sleeps. You'd like to.|You draw it out slowly and the flush leaves their face. Sister Amaranth checks your work and doesn't check twice.|You draw out the fever and the story with it. They were pushed. Sister Amaranth writes down the name.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {You draw out half of it and the half you drew comes with you. You spend the evening shivering in the next cot.|The cure has to come from somewhere. You wake up two hours later on the floor, with a blanket on you.|The fever comes out angry and goes into you. You spend the night in the next cot, insisting you weren't pushed.}
> right: Brew the slow tonic
  alchemy +small
  mood -small
  say: {Three hours of stirring willow bark and lake moss. The tonic works by morning, which is slower and safer.|You brew the old remedy by the book. Sister Amaranth tastes it, grimaces, and approves.|You brew the tonic without the book now. Sister Amaranth tastes it and doesn't grimace, which worries you more.}
> up[purify]: Distil the fever out in one pass
  study alchemy +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {You distil the fever into a single grey drop and stopper it. The drop takes some of your strength with it, and leaves you yawning.|One pass of Distil and the patient sits up asking for soup. The tiredness lands on you an hour later.|You distil it out in one pass, and your patient sits up and says who pushed them. You're asleep before Sister Amaranth asks.}

@event alch_third_floor_still
title: The Still Cracks
track: alchemy
level: 3
class: incident
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cauldron
tags: class danger
scene: A copper still on a long bench, hissing from a fresh crack, a thin green vapour creeping across the laboratory floor.
text: {Advanced Alchemy distils essences that would rather be gas. The ventilation charm gave up on the third floor years ago. Today your still cracks.|Distilling day, and a hiss from your still that you know.|Your still has cracked again. You're getting fast at noticing.} {The vapour is green and smells of mint.|The vapour is pale and smells of nothing, which is worse.|The vapour is curling toward the next bench.}
> left: Mend the crack, still hot
  challenge enchant standard+1:
    win:
      alchemy +small
      say: {You close the copper under your palms while it's still singing. The hiss stops. The class breathes out.|The crack seals with a sound like a sigh, and the batch is saved. The lecturer opens a window, for form's sake.|You seal it before the vapour reaches the next bench. Its owner never knows how close it came.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -small
      say: {The crack closes, then reopens with a pop. You breathe in a mouthful and spend the afternoon seeing double.|You burn your fingertips on the copper and the vapour gets out anyway. The lab is cleared for an hour.|The vapour reaches the next bench before you do. Two of you spend the afternoon seeing double.}
> right: Shout, and clear the bench
  study alchemy -tiny
  mood -small
  say: {You call the warning and clear the bench. The batch is lost, nobody is hurt, and you start again next week.|Everyone is out in the corridor in a minute flat. The lecturer goes back in, a scarf over the mouth, and comes out carrying your ruined batch.|You clear the bench and the one next to it. The lecturer notes that you thought of the neighbours, and the batch is still lost.}

# ---------------- Illusion & Glamour (track: illusion, skill charm) ----------------

@event illu_walking_double
title: The Walking Double
track: illusion
level: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: mirror
tags: class magic
scene: Two identical students walking side by side across a lecture hall, one of them leaving no footprints in the chalk dust.
text: {Mirror at Illusion II. Make a double of yourself and walk it across the room. "Standing doubles are furniture," says Professor Vey. "Make it walk."|Doubles day. Vey watches feet, not faces.|Your double's walk is improving. It has your slouch now.} {The floor has been dusted with chalk, to show footprints.|Someone's double is walking backwards.|Vey is sitting in the front row, which is unsettling.}
> left: Walk it right across
  challenge enchant using charm standard:
    win:
      charm +small
      say: {Your double crosses the room at your pace and even scuffs the chalk. Vey watches its feet the whole way and says nothing, which is high praise.|It walks, it turns, it nearly sits down. Vey says, "Better."|Your double slouches across the room, Vey in the front row watching every step. "That's you," Vey says. It isn't a compliment, quite.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {Halfway across, your double's legs forget to move and it glides. Vey says it's a ghost, not a person, and moves on.|Your double walks into the wall and keeps walking. The class watches it vanish into the plaster.|Your double sees Vey in the front row and freezes, exactly as you would. It's very lifelike, and it doesn't walk.}
> right: Keep it still and perfect
  study illusion +small
  mana -small
  say: {Your double stands at your elbow, perfect to the last button. It doesn't move. Vey calls it a very good wardrobe.|You make it flawless and keep it standing. Vey taps its nose, and frowns when it holds.|Your double stands perfectly still in front of Vey, slouch and all. Vey reaches out and adjusts its collar, which holds.}

@event illu_good_news
title: Good News, Read Aloud
track: illusion
level: 2
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: mask
tags: class magic
scene: A student standing at a lectern reading from a dull notice while the listening class smiles as though hearing wonderful news.
text: {Glamour at Illusion II, and today it's the voice. Vey hands you a dull notice and asks you to read it so the class hears good news.|Another notice from Vey's drawer of very dull notices.|Vey has saved the dullest notice for you.} {Today's is about the drains.|Today's is about a change to the laundry rota.|Today's is a list of overdue library books, in full.}
> left: Glamour every word
  challenge deceive using charm standard:
    win:
      charm +small
      say: {By the second line the room is beaming. Someone claps. Vey waits for the clapping to stop and asks what the notice said. Nobody knows.|You read it warm and low, and the class leans in as if you were reading their letters from home.|You read out the overdue books like a list of prize-winners. Three students go to the library that afternoon.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {Your glamour slips on a long word and the room hears exactly what it is, a notice. Someone yawns on purpose.|You overdo it. The class looks alarmed, as if the notice were about to propose.|The glamour holds for twenty titles and drops on the twenty-first. The class hears the rest exactly as they are.}
> right: Read it plain, watch others
  study illusion +small
  say: {You read it straight and sit down. Then you watch the others, and notice the best of them barely change their voices at all.|Plain reading, polite applause. You spend the hour writing down where the good ones breathe.|You read the list plain. It takes a long time. You learn more watching Vey's face than you would have by trying.}
> up[glamour]: Glamour the notice itself
  charm +small
  renown +tiny
  say: {You don't touch your voice; you glamour the paper. Every student who reads it over your shoulder sees good news in their own hand. Vey applauds, twice, slowly.|You glamour the ink so it shimmers like a wedding invitation. The class fights over the notice afterwards. It is still, underneath, a very dull notice.|You glamour the list into an honours roll. Half the class goes to look for their names, and finds their overdue books.}

@event illu_false_room
title: The Room That Isn't
track: illusion
level: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: door
tags: class magic danger
scene: A student walking confidently into a room that is partly painted light, a real stone wall standing where the illusion shows an open doorway.
text: {Advanced Illusion asks for a double of a whole room. Hang it over the real one and walk a classmate through it. Vey's rule is simple. If anyone walks into a wall, it's your fault.|The room exercise. Somebody always walks into something.|You hang your room. You've learned where the real walls are.} {Vey's volunteer today is a porter with a tray of cups.|Vey's volunteer today is Vey.|The real room has been rearranged overnight.}
> left: Hang the whole room
  challenge enchant using charm standard+1:
    win:
      charm +medium
      say: {Your walker crosses the false room and sits down in a false chair, which you hold up for them. Vey writes for a long time.|The room holds. Nobody touches a real wall. Vey walks it twice to be sure.|You hang your room over the rearranged one and nobody notices which is which, including, briefly, Vey.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {Your walker steps through a door you painted onto solid stone. You catch them as they fall, take the edge of the stone on your own elbow, and walk them to Sister Amaranth with an apology.|Halfway across the room flickers, and the walker freezes. So do you, and you walk hip-first into a real chair.|You hang your room over where the walls were yesterday. The walker finds today's walls with their nose.}
> right: Chalk the real walls first
  study illusion +small
  mana -small
  say: {You hang half a room, with the real walls marked out in chalk so nobody could miss them. Vey calls it "a room with a safety rope".|Safe and visible, chalk lines everywhere. Vey sighs, and then copies down how you did the window.|You chalk the new walls first, all of them. Vey says that's the only correct answer to a rearranged room.}

@event illu_peeling_layers
title: Seeing Through
track: illusion
level: 3
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: eye
tags: class magic
scene: A lecture hall in layers like peeled paper: one layer with velvet curtains, one bare stone, one darker one beneath, and a student standing at its centre.
text: {Advanced Illusion. To make illusions you must see through them. Vey layers the hall in false rooms, one on another, and you peel them back one at a time.|Layer day. The hall looks normal. That's never a good sign.|You count the layers when you come in. You're usually wrong.} {The top layer has velvet curtains.|The top layer is a summer garden.|The top layer is the hall itself, just slightly too tidy.}
> left: Peel down to the stone
  challenge exam standard+1:
    win:
      charm +small
      study illusion +tiny
      say: {Seven layers, then bare stone. Vey asks how many. You say seven. She smiles and says eight, and points at herself.|You reach the stone and stay there, sure of it. Vey nods slowly. "Hold on to that," she says.|The too-tidy hall comes off last, and under it is the real hall, untidy and wonderful. Vey says you're the first today.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {You peel one too many and the stone underneath looks false. For the rest of the day you knock on walls, with a headache behind your eyes.|After the fourth layer you stop being sure which way is up. Vey walks you to a chair, which is real.|You peel the tidy hall and find another tidy hall. And another. Vey stops you at the sixth, gently.}
> right: Stop at the third layer
  charm +small
  mood -small
  say: {Three layers and you stop, dizzy. Vey says knowing when to stop is the other half of the skill.|You get through three and sit down. The hall still looks too tidy, and you decide not to ask.|You stop at three, and you're right to: the third was the real one. Vey says so, with some surprise.}

# ---------------- Beastcraft & Flight (track: beastcraft, skill vigor) ----------------

@event beast_hedge_run
title: The Hedge Run
track: beastcraft
level: 2
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: broom
tags: class magic danger
scene: Students on brooms skimming low over a line of hedges in a field, one broom bucking as its rider leans forward.
text: {Beastcraft II puts haste on a broom, low over the hedges, as fast as you dare. Professor Brask says brooms are harder to reason with than hippogriffs, and harder to apologise to.|The hedge run. Your broom remembers last time.|Hedges. You've learned to read a broom's sulk through the handle.} {Brask is timing with a crooked hourglass.|The hedges have been trimmed, unevenly.|{season == "autumn": Leaves come up behind every broom.|{season == "spring": The hedges are full of nesting birds who object.|The ground is hard and unforgiving.}}}
> left: Haste at the last hedge
  challenge feat standard wild:
    win:
      vigor +small
      study beastcraft +tiny
      say: {You borrow a hawk's speed for the last hedge and clear it by a hand. Brask stops the hourglass and nods.|The broom flinches, then commits. You come in fastest of the morning, with hedge in your hair.|You feel the broom's sulk through the handle and haste it anyway, kindly. It clears the last hedge and forgives you.}
    lose:
      health -small
      say: {The broom bucks at the haste and you go over the hedge without it.|You're fast. The broom is faster, briefly, and then upside down. You land in a bramble.|The broom has been sulking all morning and saves it for the last hedge. You go over; it stays.}
> right: Fly it clean and steady
  vigor +small
  say: {No haste, just a clean low line over every hedge. Brask says it's the best boring flying she's seen all week.|You fly it slow and tidy. The broom approves. Brask is less impressed.|You fly it steady and let it sulk. Brask says the broom will remember you were patient, and so will she.}

@event beast_owl_post
title: Teaching the Owls
track: beastcraft
level: 2
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: owl
tags: class nature
scene: A student in a stable loft with a young owl on their arm, pointing out of a high window toward a distant tower.
text: {Beast-speech at Beastcraft II. The young owls must learn the post routes. Brask hands you one and says, "Explain the castle to it."|A new young owl, and the same castle to explain.|Another owl. You've got a speech now, and a map scratched on a beam.} {Your owl wants to know why there are so many towers.|Your owl has strong opinions about the library.|Your owl keeps falling asleep, standing up.}
> left: Explain it in owl
  challenge negotiate using vigor standard:
    win:
      vigor +small
      study beastcraft +tiny
      say: {You describe the castle in winds, perches and mice. The owl blinks, and goes and comes back with Brask's letter.|It takes a while to get "left" across to a bird. When it does, the owl does the route twice to show off.|You give your speech, map and all. The sleepy owl wakes for the important part and does the route without a mistake.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {The owl listens closely, then flies to the kitchens. Every time.|The owl makes you explain "tower" seven times and then goes to sleep on your head.|Your owl sleeps through the whole speech, then wakes and flies to the kitchens. You're starting to take it personally.}
> right: Walk the route with it
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {You walk the whole post route with the owl on your arm, up every stair. Your legs ache and the owl has learned it.|Up and down the castle with an owl on your wrist. People stare. The owl stares back.|You walk the route with the owl asleep on your arm. It learns it anyway, somehow. Brask says they do that.}

@event beast_hooves
title: Hoof Day
track: beastcraft
level: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: paw
tags: class nature danger
scene: A student crouched beside a hippogriff in a stall, a hoof trimming knife in hand, the hippogriff looking down with one sceptical eye.
text: {Advanced Beastcraft does the work nobody else wants. Today it's hooves. A hippogriff's need trimming each month, and the hippogriff disagrees.|Hoof day. The stall is the same; the hippogriff's opinion is too.|Hooves. You go in with a bucket of apples and a plan.} {Today's hippogriff is grey and patient.|Today's is young and very sure of itself.|Today's is old and wants to tell you about it.}
> left: Talk it into lifting a foot
  challenge negotiate using vigor standard+1 wild:
    win:
      vigor +small
      study beastcraft +small
      say: {It listens to your case and lifts a hoof with enormous dignity. Brask watches from the door and says nothing.|You talk it through every hoof. It sighs at you between each one, like a patient uncle.|The old hippogriff tells you about its youth for every hoof. You listen to all of it, and it lifts each foot when the story ends.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -tiny
      say: {It lets you get all the way to the last hoof before stepping on your foot.|It hears you out, then sits on you. Brask lifts it off with one hand and a word.|The old one talks and talks and doesn't lift a foot. You've been had, and Brask is laughing at the door.}
> right: Rope, and Brask holding
  vigor +small
  health -small
  say: {The old way, then. A rope, a strong arm, Brask at its head. It's sweaty work and nobody enjoys it, including the hippogriff.|With Brask holding the head and you holding on, you finish every hoof. Your shoulders will remember it.|Apples first, then rope, then Brask. The plan works, and the hippogriff eats the plan.}

@event beast_night_stable
title: Sitting Up With the Sick
track: beastcraft
level: 3
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: lantern
tags: class nature
scene: A student sitting in straw by lantern light, a sick creature curled beside them, the rest of the stables asleep.
text: {Advanced students take turns sitting up in the stables when a creature is ill. At the morning lecture Brask hands you a lantern, a blanket and a list for tonight.|Your name is on Brask's night-watch list again, and the list is shorter.|Night watch again tonight. Brask hands over the lantern without a word; the owls will only mutter.} {This time it's the griffon chick, off its food.|This time it's an old owl with a cough.|This time it's a hippogriff who ate something it shouldn't.} {season == "winter": The stable will be cold enough to see your breath.|At least it's warm in the straw.}
> left: Sit up all night
  vigor +small
  study beastcraft +small
  health -small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You don't sleep, and by dawn it's breathing easier. Brask finds you asleep in the straw and leaves you there.|You watch it all night, changing the water and the straw. In the morning it eats, and so do you, enormously.|You sit up with the hippogriff while it groans about its mistake. By dawn it's eating again, and you're asleep against its side.}
> right: Ask it where it hurts
  challenge negotiate using vigor standard+1:
    win:
      vigor +small
      say: {It tells you, in its own way, exactly where it hurts. You write it on Brask's list and both of you sleep.|It points with its whole body. You bring the right tonic and it drops off before the lantern does.|It tells you exactly what it ate, with some pride. You fetch the purge and it forgives you by morning.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: {It tells you a great deal, about its neighbours, the hay and the moon, and nothing about where it hurts.|It won't talk to you. It talks to the lantern. You stay up anyway, which is the point.|It won't say what it ate, out of embarrassment. You guess, and Brask corrects the guess in the morning.}
> up[mend]: Mend what hurts
  study beastcraft +small
  say: {You find the sore place with your hands and mend it. By midnight it's breathing easier, and so are you.|You mend the cough out of its chest, slowly, a breath at a time. It sleeps before the lantern does, and so do you.|You mend the ache in its belly. It looks at you, ashamed and grateful, and Brask's lecture in the morning is short.}

# ---------------- Umbral Theory (track: umbral, skill guile) ----------------

@event umbral_ward_candle
title: Ward the Candle
track: umbral
level: 2
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: candle
tags: class magic social
cast:
  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)
scene: Two students at a dim bench under low lamps, a candle between them inside a faint pale shell, one student trying to blow it out.
text: {Ward at Umbral Theory II. Ward a candle while your partner tries to put it out. Today that's {partner}.|Candle wards again, and the lamps are lower. {partner} is across the bench.|The candle exercise. You're paired with {partner}, who rolls up {their} sleeves.} {The professor walks between the benches with a hand over the flames.|The room smells of wax and burnt thread.|Someone's ward has shrunk to the size of a thimble.}
> left: A thick, honest shell
  guile +small
  mana -small
  rel partner +tiny
  say: {Your ward is plain and solid. {partner} blows and blows and the candle doesn't even lean.|Strong and dull. {partner} gives up and the two of you watch the flame burn down.|Your shell is thick and calm. {partner}'s sleeves are rolled for nothing; the flame doesn't even notice {them}.}
> right: A ward that turns them aside
  challenge deceive standard:
    win:
      guile +small
      study umbral +tiny
      say: {Your ward turns {partner}'s breath round, and it blows {their} own hair into {their} eyes. The professor stops and says, "Ah."|{partner} reaches for your candle and finds {their} hand on the wrong side of the bench. The professor approves.|Your ward turns {partner} aside so gently that {they} try{s} three times before working out what happened.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      rel partner -tiny
      say: {Your clever ward turns aside the wrong thing, and the candle goes out with a smug little puff. {partner} smirks.|The ward bends {partner}'s breath straight into your face. You sneeze and the candle goes out.|Your ward shrinks to a thimble, like your neighbour's, and {partner} pinches the flame out with two fingers.}
> up[ward]: A true ward
  guile +small
  study umbral +small
  rel partner +tiny
  say: {You cast the real thing, the spell itself, and the candle sits inside a pale shell that hums. {partner} blows, then laughs, then asks you to show {them} how.|You raise the true ward. The professor stops at your bench and holds a hand over it, and for once says nothing at all.|The true ward comes easily now. {partner} tries everything, a breath, a book, a wet finger, and the flame burns on, untroubled.}

@event umbral_dark_roll
title: Who Moved
track: umbral
level: 2
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: moon
tags: class magic
scene: A pitch-dark classroom at the top of a tower, a few vague shapes of students sitting perfectly still in the gloom.
text: {Umbral Theory II. The professor puts out every lamp, and in the dark one student moves. Say who, and where to.|Lamps out. You've learned to hear a sleeve.|The dark. You know the room's sounds, and which floorboard is a liar.} {Rain on the tower roof makes it harder.|It's so quiet you can hear the stair breathing.|Somebody's stomach rumbles, which helps no one.}
> left: Listen, and name them
  guile +small
  say: {"Third bench, to the window." Right. The professor relights a lamp and moves on.|You get it on the second try, by the creak of a bench and a faint smell of soap.|"Second bench, toward the door, stepping over the liar." The professor laughs in the dark, once.}
> right: Be the one who moves
  challenge deceive standard:
    win:
      guile +small
      study umbral +tiny
      say: {You cross the whole room in the dark and nobody names you. When the lamps come up you're behind the professor.|Nobody hears you. The professor says, "Someone moved," and seems pleased not to know who.|You step over the floorboard that lies and cross the room in silence. Even the professor guesses wrong.}
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {You catch a desk with your shin. "Window, limping," says everyone at once.|Your shoe squeaks on the second step, you turn your ankle stopping, and the whole class names you, with feeling.|You avoid the liar and find a new one. The whole room names you before you've reached the wall.}

@event umbral_corridor_step
title: Across the Corridor
track: umbral
level: 3
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: ghost
tags: class magic danger
scene: A dim stone corridor at the top of a tower, a student half stepping out of the shadow of one doorway while their own shadow lingers in another across the hall.
text: {Advanced Umbral Theory. Shadowstep across the corridor, door to door, the farthest the professor allows. "Short distances only," the professor says. "This is as short as long gets."|The corridor. The cold in between is colder than last time.|You stand at the door with your heels on the line. You know the cold that's coming.} {The lamps are out on the landing.|A draught is moving along the floor.|Someone came back from it with frost on one shoulder.}
> left: Step across
  challenge deceive standard+1 wild:
    win:
      guile +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {One shadow, cold and long, and then the other. You come out on the far side shaking and grinning.|You step in by one door and out by the other, with a breath of frost on your collar. The professor writes your name, and a time.|You step into the cold you knew was coming and out the far door with nothing on your shoulder. The professor writes a shorter time.}
    lose:
      health -small
      mood -small
      say: {The cold in between is longer than you thought. You come out in the right doorway and can't feel your hands for an hour.|You step and come out in the same doorway you went in by, with the feeling that something looked at you on the way.|You come back with frost on one shoulder, like the others. It takes until supper to melt, and it isn't only cold.}
> right: Walk it, counting shadows
  study umbral +small
  say: {You walk the corridor and count every shadow and every lamp. The professor calls this "mapping your road".|You pace it out and write down each shadow's depth. Next time you'll step it. Probably.|You walk it and count the shadows, again. They're the same as last time, which you now realise is the lesson.}
> up[shadowstep]: Step with a held breath
  guile +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {You step without thinking about it, the way the spell knows. The cold barely touches you.|The spell takes you over quick and clean. Your fingers are cold for a minute.|You hold your breath and step. No frost, no cold, no time at all. The professor stops writing.}

@event umbral_unpick_ward
title: Unpicking the Box
track: umbral
level: 3
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: lock
tags: class magic
scene: A small iron-bound box on a bench under a dim lamp, faint threads of a pale ward wrapped around it, a student leaning close with a needle.
text: {Advanced Umbral Theory. The professor wards a small box and you unpick the ward without it noticing. "A ward that notices you," the professor says, "tells someone."|Box day. The professor has a new box, and a new ward on it.|The box. You've got a favourite needle for this now.} {This ward is fine as a cobweb.|This ward is thick and patient.|This ward hums when you breathe on it.}
> left: Thread by thread
  guile +small
  mana -small
  mood -tiny
  say: {An hour with a needle and a steady breath, and the box opens. The professor checks the ward and it has no idea.|You undo it strand by strand. It takes the whole lesson, and you don't look up once.|Your favourite needle and a slow breath. The humming ward goes quiet one strand at a time, and never notices it's gone.}
> right: Cut it in one stroke
  challenge deceive standard+1:
    win:
      guile +small
      study umbral +small
      say: {You find the knot and cut it clean. The ward never knows. The professor raises a brow and opens the box.|One cut and the threads fall away. The professor spends a moment looking at the box and then at you.|You cut it between hums. The ward stops mid-note and never starts again. The professor writes something down.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {The ward notices, snaps across your knuckles and rings like a little bell. The professor says, "And now someone knows." Everyone looks at you.|The ward snaps back at your hand and stings. The box stays shut.|You cut it mid-hum. It shrieks instead, and the professor has to open the window to let the sound out.}
